From: rive
Subject: [PATCH 0042] nest calls from other exec envs under hw bounds

With hardware bounds checks the signal handler finds the running call
through a per thread exec env, and a call on any other exec env while one
was running on the thread was refused as "invalid exec env". Natives run
other instances inside a call: a job joined on the calling thread, a file a
script decodes, a lane booted from a call. Software bounds builds have no
such limit. A call now makes its exec env the thread's for its duration and
puts the outer one back when it returns, trapped or not, so a fault unwinds
to the innermost call. The stack overflow check now runs before the exec env
is published, so its early return leaves nothing stale behind.

Starting an instance, and its malloc and free exports, reused whatever exec
env was running on the thread, swapping its instance for the call. Restoring
it passed the inner instance's trap on to the outer one, failing a call that
software bounds builds keep. Starting an instance now always makes its own
exec env, and malloc and free reuse only one of the same instance, as
software bounds builds do.

